# A step that writes a table and reads it back, and hands on what it read: the read is of the table as `keep` left it,
# inside the step, so the table keeps what `keep` leaves there, in the codomain (rule 4, as `readback-one-body`);
# what was read goes back to `run`, which prints it.

route ledger.run
step run/Ledger()
step run/Ledger.keep
step run/Ledger.keep/connect                                # `con = duckdb.connect(..)` names what it gives

line entry > run call
line run/Ledger() > Ledger@run/Ledger() value
known #126 line Ledger@run/Ledger() > run/Ledger.keep value   # its own body changes it (`self.kept +=`) besides handing it to connect
line run/Ledger.keep > Ledger@run/Ledger.keep value         # keep changes the ledger it runs on (`self.kept +=`)
line run/Ledger.keep/connect > DuckDBPyConnection·con@run/Ledger.keep/connect value
line Ledger@run/Ledger() > run/Ledger.keep/connect value   # `duckdb.connect(self.path)` hands the ledger
known #101 line DuckDBPyConnection·con@run/Ledger.keep/connect > run/Ledger.keep value   # keep's own calls use con
line run/Ledger.keep > DuckDB(notes) own
line run/Ledger.keep > run back  # the notes read back, a list of str, go back to run
line run > Screen own

home Ledger@run/Ledger() run
home Ledger@run/Ledger.keep run
home DuckDBPyConnection·con@run/Ledger.keep/connect run/Ledger.keep

side entry left
side DuckDB(notes) right # Atlas: between, through the reach below
side Screen right

# K: a value handed through a field reaches no step inside: `duckdb.connect(self.path)` hands `self` (#121, decision 1,
#    cause 3: the reader's `held_in` skips `self` even inside an argument)
# building the ledger runs its public methods, which touch the table: two steps at it, so it stands between them
